%T A400509 849,971,1108,1239,1405,1567,1747,1935,2149,2367,2597,2849,3111,3398,
%U A400509 3695,3999,4340,4692,5053,5444,5852,6269,6720,7173,7682,8184,8722,9253,9849,10448
%N A400509 a(n) is the number of distinct integer-sided triangles that can be placed with their vertices on three different sides of a square of side n.
%C A400509 A vertex at a corner is counted on only one of its two incident sides.
%C A400509 The equilateral triangle (n, n, n) is always counted.
%C A400509 For every fixed triangle, if such a placement is possible for one square size, then it is possible for every larger square up to the length of the longest side.
%C A400509 In every admissible placement, one side q joins opposite sides of the square, so n <= q <= sqrt(2)*n; if r and s are the other sides, H = sqrt((q + r + s)*(-q + r + s)*(q - r + s)*(q + r - s)), U = q^2 + r^2 - s^2, V = q^2 + s^2 - r^2, and t = sqrt(q^2 - n^2)/n, then, after possibly interchanging r and s, such a placement exists if and only if 0 <= U + H*t <= 2*q^2, 0 <= H - U*t <= 2*q^2, and 0 <= H + V*t <= 2*q^2.
%H A400509 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400509/b400509.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..400</a>.
%H A400509 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400509/a400509.pdf">Illustration of the term a(3) = 6</a>.
%H A400509 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400509/a400509.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A400509 Felix Huber, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23057791">Triangles Touching Three Sides of a Square</a>, Sep 30 2026.
%H A400509 R. P. Jerrard and J. E. Wetzel, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/S000170300009">Triangles in squares</a>, Elem. Math. 58 (2003), 65-72.
%F A400509 a(n) = C*n^3 + O(n^2), where C = lim_{n->infinity} a(n)/n^3 = 0.0776924549....
%e A400509 For n = 1, in the unit square [0,1]^2, the points (1 - sqrt(3)/2, 0), (1 - sqrt(3)/2, 1), and (1, 1/2) form an equilateral triangle of side length 1 with its vertices on three sides. Hence a(1) = 1.
%e A400509 For n = 3, the admissible triangles are (1, 3, 3), (2, 2, 3), (2, 3, 3), (2, 3, 4), (3, 3, 3), and (3, 3, 4). Hence a(3) = 6.
%p A400509 # See Huber link.
